16:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1d",null,{"guide":{"date":"February 22–March 6, 1862","place":"Fayetteville, Elm Springs, Bentonville, Little Sugar Creek, and Pea Ridge in northwest Arkansas","result":"Van Dorn failed to trap Franz Sigel at Bentonville. Curtis concentrated the Army of the Southwest behind prepared defenses at Little Sugar Creek, but Van Dorn rejected a direct attack and ordered an overnight march around the United States right flank by the Bentonville Detour","facts":[{"label":"Confederate march began","value":"March 4, 1862"},{"label":"Planned march","value":"About 38 miles from the Boston Mountain camps through Fayetteville to Bentonville"},{"label":"Load ordered per soldier","value":"Musket, 40 cartridges, one blanket, and three days’ rations"},{"label":"Sigel’s rear guard","value":"About 600 men after the 2nd Missouri left Bentonville"},{"label":"Vandever’s march","value":"42 miles in 16 hours to reach Little Sugar Creek"},{"label":"Bentonville Detour","value":"About eight miles around the United States right to Cross Timber Hollow"}],"maps":[{"title":"Bentonville and Little Sugar Creek today","caption":"Van Dorn marched north through Fayetteville and Elm Springs toward Bentonville. Sigel escaped east and joined Curtis on the north bank of Little Sugar Creek, south of the Pea Ridge battlefield.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-94.45%2C36.12%2C-93.88%2C36.55&layer=mapnik&marker=36.454%2C-94.034"},{"title":"The Bentonville Detour","caption":"The National Park Service map shows the United States position behind Little Sugar Creek and the route Van Dorn selected around the army’s right. The overnight movement aimed to reach the Telegraph Road in Curtis’s rear.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/bentonville-detour.htm","image":"/images/pea-ridge-bentonville-detour.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Bentonville Detour and the armies near Little Sugar Creek and Pea Ridge"},{"title":"The approach from Fayetteville","caption":"The broader campaign map shows Van Dorn’s route north from the Boston Mountains through Fayetteville and Bentonville. Curtis’s supply route continued north through Springfield toward Rolla.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/the-pea-ridge-campaign.htm","image":"/images/pea-ridge-campaign-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Pea Ridge campaign in Missouri and Arkansas"},{"title":"Roads and terrain at Pea Ridge","caption":"The 1876 U.S. Army map identifies the Telegraph Road, Cross Timber Hollow, Elkhorn Tavern, Leetown, and the ridge. Van Dorn’s night march was intended to place his army across the Telegraph Road north of Curtis.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447263/","image":"/images/pea-ridge-battlefield-map.jpg","alt":"1876 map of the Pea Ridge battlefield showing roads, settlements, terrain, and troop positions"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:09","audioTime":69,"title":"Curtis held his position in northwest Arkansas","detail":"Curtis had driven Price out of Missouri. By March 1, he decided to remain in Arkansas, concentrate his divided army, and prepare for a Confederate advance."},{"time":"3:02","audioTime":182,"title":"Van Dorn left Little Rock for the army","detail":"News of Price’s retreat reached Van Dorn on February 22. Within two days he left Little Rock with a small staff and traveled toward the Confederate camps in the Boston Mountains."},{"time":"3:34","audioTime":214,"title":"Van Dorn became ill on the journey","detail":"After falling into the Little Red River, Van Dorn reached Van Buren on March 1 with chills and fever. McCulloch reported that the army was ready to march."},{"time":"4:15","audioTime":255,"title":"Van Dorn reached Price’s headquarters","detail":"Van Dorn arrived on March 2. Price’s men had spent a week resting and drawing supplies in the Boston Mountain camps after their retreat from Missouri."},{"time":"4:51","audioTime":291,"title":"Confederate cavalry reported the United States army dispersed","detail":"Price and McCulloch briefed Van Dorn on March 3. Their cavalry had tracked Curtis’s divisions across northwest Arkansas."},{"time":"5:05","audioTime":305,"title":"Van Dorn decided to strike Sigel at Bentonville","detail":"Van Dorn believed he could reach the two divisions around Bentonville with a three-to-one advantage, defeat them, and then turn against Curtis’s remaining divisions."},{"time":"5:33","audioTime":333,"title":"Speed replaced logistical preparation","detail":"Van Dorn ordered the army to leave tents and baggage behind. Each man carried a musket, 40 cartridges, one blanket, and three days’ rations; the ammunition and supply train would follow."},{"time":"6:22","audioTime":382,"title":"The Confederate march began in a snowstorm","detail":"On March 4, the army left its camps for Fayetteville and Bentonville. Snow, sleet, mud, hunger, and little sleep weakened men and animals during the march."},{"time":"6:56","audioTime":416,"title":"Curtis had already ordered Sigel to withdraw","detail":"On March 3, Curtis directed Sigel’s divisions to leave Bentonville and join the prepared position at Little Sugar Creek. Sigel initially moved without urgency."},{"time":"7:48","audioTime":468,"title":"Van Dorn’s army advanced from Fayetteville","detail":"Price’s command led the March 5 movement up the Elm Springs Road. Van Dorn, still ill, traveled in an ambulance while the troops marched through freezing conditions."},{"time":"8:28","audioTime":508,"title":"Sigel withdrew the Elm Springs outpost","detail":"As Sigel prepared to leave Bentonville, his cavalry outpost at Elm Springs returned to town. The withdrawal temporarily left the Confederate approach unguarded."},{"time":"10:05","audioTime":605,"title":"The Confederates camped at Elm Springs","detail":"Van Dorn’s troops reached Elm Springs late on March 5. The 3rd Texas Cavalry continued north and encountered pickets left by Colonel Frederick Schaefer."},{"time":"11:16","audioTime":676,"title":"Curtis learned that the Confederate army was moving north","detail":"A United States supporter from Fayetteville and one of Curtis’s spies independently reported the advance. Curtis sent Sigel an urgent order to march for Little Sugar Creek."},{"time":"11:59","audioTime":719,"title":"Sigel ordered the Bentonville withdrawal","detail":"At 1 a.m. on March 6, Sigel sent Alexander Asboth’s division and the wagon train first, followed by Peter Osterhaus’s division. Most of the column cleared town by mid-morning."},{"time":"12:36","audioTime":756,"title":"A missed order cut Sigel’s rear guard in half","detail":"Sigel intended to combine 600 men in town with Schaefer’s 2nd Missouri. Schaefer did not receive the instruction and marched away, leaving Sigel with about 600 men."},{"time":"13:23","audioTime":803,"title":"Van Dorn split his approach to Bentonville","detail":"He sent most of the cavalry northwest by Osage Mills while the infantry continued up the Bentonville Road. The intended two-direction attack began after most of Sigel’s force had escaped."},{"time":"14:43","audioTime":883,"title":"Confederate cavalry converged south of Bentonville","detail":"At about 10 a.m., Sigel learned his planned reinforcement had left and that Confederates were close. James McIntosh’s and Elijah Gates’s cavalry commands joined south of town."},{"time":"15:33","audioTime":933,"title":"Sigel fought a rear-guard action eastward","detail":"McIntosh sent Gates directly after Sigel while taking Texas cavalry through and around Bentonville. Cold, fatigue, and exhausted horses slowed the attempted encirclement."},{"time":"17:03","audioTime":1023,"title":"Sigel escaped to Curtis","detail":"Sigel managed the retreat under repeated cavalry attacks and reached the main army. By the evening of March 6, Curtis had concentrated all four divisions."},{"time":"17:27","audioTime":1047,"title":"Vandever completed a forced march","detail":"Colonel William Vandever’s detachment reached Little Sugar Creek after marching 42 miles in 16 hours over icy roads without losing a man."},{"time":"17:56","audioTime":1076,"title":"Curtis prepared the Little Sugar Creek position","detail":"United States infantry and artillery occupied bluffs on the north bank above the creek. A direct attack would have required Confederates to cross the valley under downward fire."},{"time":"18:32","audioTime":1112,"title":"Van Dorn reached Bentonville with an exhausted army","detail":"After three days of marching, food and forage were gone and straggling was widespread. Van Dorn still believed Curtis was retreating rather than concentrating to fight."},{"time":"19:29","audioTime":1169,"title":"Albert Pike’s command arrived","detail":"The 1st and 2nd Cherokee Mounted Rifles and a squadron of Texas cavalry joined Van Dorn late on March 6."},{"time":"19:43","audioTime":1183,"title":"McCulloch proposed turning the United States right","detail":"At a 5 p.m. command meeting, McCulloch advised against attacking Little Sugar Creek. He proposed using the Bentonville Detour to force Curtis out of the position."},{"time":"20:25","audioTime":1225,"title":"Van Dorn ordered an immediate night march","detail":"McCulloch asked to let the exhausted army rest. Van Dorn refused and ordered the column onto the detour that evening."},{"time":"21:08","audioTime":1268,"title":"Van Dorn changed the flanking move into an envelopment","detail":"Price and McCulloch wanted a limited move that would force Curtis toward Missouri. Van Dorn instead aimed to reach the Telegraph Road in Curtis’s rear and compel the entire army to surrender."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Samuel R. Curtis","command":"Army of the Southwest","subordinates":"Concentrated four divisions at Little Sugar Creek"},{"commander":"Franz Sigel","command":"1st and 2nd Divisions","subordinates":"Peter J. Osterhaus and Alexander Asboth"},{"commander":"Jefferson C. Davis","command":"3rd Division","subordinates":"Already with Curtis near Little Sugar Creek"},{"commander":"Eugene A. Carr","command":"4th Division","subordinates":"Already with Curtis near Little Sugar Creek"},{"commander":"Frederick Schaefer","command":"2nd Missouri Infantry","subordinates":"Left pickets on the Elm Springs Road and then marched from Bentonville"},{"commander":"William Vandever","command":"Detached column","subordinates":"Made the final 42-mile march to concentrate with Curtis"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"Earl Van Dorn","command":"Army of the West","subordinates":"Overall command and plan for the envelopment"},{"commander":"Sterling Price","command":"Missouri State Guard","subordinates":"Led the march north from Fayetteville"},{"commander":"Benjamin McCulloch","command":"McCulloch’s Division","subordinates":"Proposed the Bentonville Detour"},{"commander":"James McIntosh","command":"Confederate cavalry","subordinates":"Directed the pursuit from Bentonville"},{"commander":"Elijah Gates","command":"Missouri cavalry","subordinates":"Pressed Sigel’s rear guard across the prairie"},{"commander":"Albert Pike","command":"Indian Territory brigade","subordinates":"1st and 2nd Cherokee Mounted Rifles and Texas cavalry"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Earl Van Dorn","role":"Ordered the rapid advance and the overnight envelopment by the Bentonville Detour","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/earl-van-dorn.jpg","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/general-van-dorn.htm"},{"name":"Samuel R. Curtis","role":"Concentrated the Army of the Southwest at Little Sugar Creek and prepared the defensive position","side":"United States","image":"/images/samuel-r-curtis.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018669095/"},{"name":"Franz Sigel","role":"Withdrew the 1st and 2nd Divisions from Bentonville and conducted the final rear-guard action","side":"United States","image":"/images/franz-sigel.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666489/"},{"name":"Benjamin McCulloch","role":"Advised against a direct attack and proposed using the Bentonville Detour","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James McIntosh","role":"Led Confederate cavalry around Bentonville but failed to cut off Sigel","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Frederick Schaefer","role":"Left the pickets that detected the Confederate advance on the Elm Springs Road","side":"United States"},{"name":"William Vandever","role":"Led the last United States detachment into the Little Sugar Creek position","side":"United States"},{"name":"Albert Pike","role":"Brought the Cherokee Mounted Rifles and Texas cavalry into Van Dorn’s army","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/pea-ridge-battle-print.jpg","alt":"1889 chromolithograph of United States and Confederate troops fighting at Pea Ridge","caption":"This 1889 print includes American Indian troops. Albert Pike’s Cherokee regiments joined Van Dorn at the end of the episode.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2013645343/"},{"image":"/images/pea-ridge-bentonville-detour.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Bentonville Detour","caption":"Van Dorn ordered the exhausted army onto this route during the night of March 6–7.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/bentonville-detour.htm"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 102","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/102-pea-ridge-part-the-second"},{"label":"National Park Service: The Pea Ridge Campaign","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/the-pea-ridge-campaign.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Bentonville Detour","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/bentonville-detour.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Pea Ridge order of battle","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/order-of-battle.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Samuel R. Curtis","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/curtis.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Earl Van Dorn","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/general-van-dorn.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Pea Ridge battlefield map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447263/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Battle of Pea Ridge print","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2013645343/"}]}}]}]
